{:edb/db-before-t 1284 :edb/db-after-t 1285 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534597 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:59:18.125Z" 13194139534597 true] [17592186128938 1100 "What are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1982/Source/Images/f1f3dd486bff704208c9340c8937defb.png)" 13194139534597 false] [17592186128938 1100 "What are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/02219461392f94791f20e3e7f0e125e846b602862524b381fa909530e0f4e9d6.png)" 13194139534597 true] [17592186128938 1145 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n$$\n\\rho \\geq 0\n$$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)\n$$" 13194139534597 false] [17592186128938 1145 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where $\\rho \\geq 0,$ $0 \\leq \\theta <   2\\pi,$ and $0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following: \n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186128939 1100 "The point $Q$ has spherical coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt3, \\pi, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$." 13194139534597 false] [17592186128939 1100 "The point $Q$ has spherical coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt3, \\pi, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186128939 1145 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-\\sqrt3, 0, -3).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186128939 1145 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos (π)\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\sin (π)\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= ρ\\cos ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-\\sqrt3, 0, -3)$." 13194139534597 false] [17592186128940 1100 "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2)$. What are the spherical polar coordinates of $P$?" 13194139534597 false] [17592186128940 1100 "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of  $P?$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186128940 1145 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+0+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)$ lies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, for $\\phi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2, \\pi,  \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186128940 1145 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad \\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2} + 0^{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 + 0 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)\n$$\n\nlies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\phi$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ &= \\arccos (\\frac{z}{ρ}) \\\\\n&= \\arccos (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2, \\pi, \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right)\n$$" 13194139534597 false] [17592186129119 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297892 13194139534597 true] [17592186129119 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297899 13194139534597 true] [17592186129119 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297906 13194139534597 true] [17592186129120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297878 13194139534597 true] [17592186129120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297919 13194139534597 true] [17592186129120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297926 13194139534597 true] [17592186129120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297933 13194139534597 true] [17592186129120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297940 13194139534597 true] [17592186129121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297885 13194139534597 true] [17592186129121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297947 13194139534597 true] [17592186129121 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297954 13194139534597 true] [17592186129800 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534597 true] [17592186129800 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/a9f9418d0a9984af019254cb06d9b2aab3251dd3127f05b89b118620ac6dd7e2.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $A?$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186129800 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1982/Source/Images/q-74320.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $A$?" 13194139534597 false] [17592186129800 1145 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $A$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $A$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $A$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186129803 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534597 true] [17592186129803 1100 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-1, \\sqrt{2})$. What are the spherical polar coordinates of $A$?" 13194139534597 false] [17592186129803 1100 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-1,\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $A?$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186129803 1145 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186129804 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534597 true] [17592186129804 1100 "The point $B$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1, 2,-\\sqrt{3})$. What are the spherical polar coordinates $(ρ, θ, ϕ)$ of $B$? Express $θ$ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." 13194139534597 false] [17592186129804 1100 "The point $B$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,2,-\\sqrt{3}).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $B?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." 13194139534597 true] [17592186129804 1145 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ lies in the second quadrant. So to find $\\theta $, we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{2}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & ≈1.107\\,149\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -1.107\\,149 \\\\  & ≈2.034\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}}) \\\\  & ≈2.230.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{5},2.034,2.230).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186129806 1017 #edb/ref 17592186128630 13194139534597 false] [17592186129806 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297913 13194139534597 true] [17592186129806 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534597 true] [17592186129806 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/561430ec00f405b6cce3ab743de1665fce8909f0a505bcd0df05478badafae35.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $D?$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186129806 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1982/Source/Images/q-74543.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $D$?" 13194139534597 false] [17592186129806 1145 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $D$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $D$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $D$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $D$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297879 13194139534597 true] [17592186297878 1089 "q-165297" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297878 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534597 true] [17592186297878 1100 "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297878 1113 #uuid "613c94d2-7fac-5e2b-b2b7-18ae60e90291" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297878 1145 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297880 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297881 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297882 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297883 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297884 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1041 "selection" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297882 13194139534597 true] [17592186297879 1110 #uuid "47cdb975-c902-50e4-a23b-e2e40accd173" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297880 1034 "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534597 true] [17592186297880 1111 #uuid "2ea84e8a-6615-5b37-bbb0-41c46a77c690" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297881 1034 "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534597 true] [17592186297881 1111 #uuid "3e6607f5-0190-5b4b-a365-040cd221d307" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297882 1034 "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534597 true] [17592186297882 1111 #uuid "5c532576-052c-54d6-894f-a8b076a54904" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297883 1034 "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297883 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534597 true] [17592186297883 1111 #uuid "20ac82b6-7e80-5bdd-82fc-ac2763b575f9" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297884 1034 "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534597 true] [17592186297884 1111 #uuid "f7c182ad-48b0-5a5a-9373-6746255eb836" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297885 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297886 13194139534597 true] [17592186297885 1089 "q-165299" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297885 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534597 true] [17592186297885 1100 "The point $K$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $K?$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297885 1113 #uuid "5090116f-465b-5350-9f1c-5fb94addf706" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297885 1145 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297887 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297888 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297889 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297890 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297891 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1041 "selection" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297889 13194139534597 true] [17592186297886 1110 #uuid "0fde7d56-8839-5098-8c70-ac6f48c3ae9e" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297887 1034 "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534597 true] [17592186297887 1111 #uuid "82954505-9375-5455-87de-2f1bcaa17553" 13194139534597 true] [17592186297888 1034 "(2,\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" 13194139534597 true] [1759218629